From 83cf14ea964dc1bb4877c87d35a705946e9971ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Kinney <mike.kinney@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 01:31:30 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix(post): each mill post carries a flipped WCS axis and
 an inverted machine axis

The engine mirrors a section with a flipped WCS X or Y (#1649) and a
program for a machine with an inverted X or Y (#374). Only fanuc.cps and
haas.cps declared `axisInversion = true`, so each other mill script
refused the two cases by name.

Mazak, Doosan DNM, Siemens 840D, Heidenhain, Mach4, grblHAL, Tormach,
TinyG, Carvera and LinuxCNC now declare the flag. Each writes the
section note `WCS X FLIPPED - PROGRAM MIRRORED` and the header note
`MACHINE X+ MOVES LEFT - INVERTED AXIS` in its comment form.

The proof is crates/post/tests/wcs_flip_dialects.rs with the new reader
tests/mirror_trace. The reader reads each posted program as a controller
whose X, Y, or X and Y run reversed. It compares the physical cut runs
with the CAM path point by point, the physical compensation side, and
the rapid end points. It reads native arcs, split arcs, chords, R-word
arcs, a helix, G81, the Heidenhain G200 / M99 call and expanded holes.
The standard program on the reversed controller must stray.

Probing stays refused: in a flipped WCS (engine), and on an inverted
machine axis through the ten scripts (no probeAxisInversion).

The refusal hint "(Fanuc, Haas)" is now "(each built-in mill post
does)". The refusal tests use a lathe script and a mill script with the
flag removed.

Fixes #2475

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
